      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola",
      "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-4$ and $k=2.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-4,2).$",
